    summary_en: "Setracker2 Android Companion App (com.tgelec.setracker), versions 3.1.5 and earlier, uses the MD5 algorithm to generate request signatures when communicating with the backend REST API. This vulnerability allows attackers to potentially reverse-engineer the signature generation mechanism, thereby recovering a valid Session ID. Once the Session ID is stolen, the attacker can impersonate a legitimate user and issue authenticated API requests, effectively causing unauthorized privilege escalation. Although the original text does not provide a CVSS score or a patched version, users are advised to immediately upgrade to a stable version later than 3.1.5, and to consider reviewing the application's communication security mechanisms, avoiding the use of outdated and less secure hashing algorithms like MD5 for authentication. Developers should consider adopting stronger, more secure encryption standards to protect session IDs and API authorization.",
